# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/fixtures.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event amaranth_intro
# en: No Excuses
title [8daa28] = 不许找借口
# en: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. "Better," she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.
text [2645db] = 你本来只是来要块膏药。阿玛兰斯修女拉过你的手，对着光转了转，问你怎么弄的。你告诉了她。她等着。你又把真实的经过讲了一遍。“这还差不多，”她说着，伸手去拿一个瓶子——不是膏药。
# en: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.
scene [fc4802] = 一位系着白围裙的严厉治疗师，把一名学生的手腕举到窗前的光里，身后摆着一排补剂瓶。
# en: Drink whatever it is
opt.left.label [6cbe5e] = 不管是什么，喝了
# en: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. "Come back if it swells," she calls. "Don't come back if it doesn't."
opt.left.say.1 [9fbd34] = 一股铁锈和后悔的味道。还没走到门口，手就不疼了。“肿了再来，”她在身后喊，“不肿就别来。”
# en: Just the plaster, thanks
opt.right.label [1353de] = 给块膏药就行，谢谢
# en: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. "Three crowns," she says. "The look is free."
opt.right.say.1 [606886] = 她给了你膏药，外加一个眼神——下次你再想对治疗师撒谎时，一定会想起它。“三克朗，”她说，“眼神免费。”

@event amaranth_rounds
# en: Evening Rounds
title [ee9b30] = 晚间查房
# en: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {Six beds on the ward today.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}
text [985a3b] = {阿玛兰斯修女在走廊里拦住你，一言不发地把一盘补剂塞到你手里。|你路过时，阿玛兰斯把补剂托盘递了过来。看来你已经上了值班表。|托盘在病房桌上等着你，上面贴着阿玛兰斯方方正正的字：“你的”。} {今天病房里六张床。|一个一年级生，打嗝会发光。|有人把自己的眉毛给咒没了。}
# en: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.
scene [9f326b] = 黄昏时分，一位治疗师和一名学生走在长长的病房里，学生端着一盘小玻璃瓶，病床边的灯一盏盏点亮。
# en: Do the rounds with her
opt.left.label [640527] = 跟她一起查房
# en: {She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. "Again," she says. You do it again. "Fine."}
opt.left.say.1 [24b737] = {她告诉你每种补剂的用处，以及为什么是这个味道。出乎你自己意料，你全记住了。|你弄明白了哪些病人勇敢、哪些病人吵闹，而这两种人很少是同一批。|到最后一张床，她让你量剂量。“再来，”她说。你又量了一遍。“行。”}
# en: You have an essay due
opt.right.label [427f10] = 你还有篇论文要交
# en: {"So does everyone," she says, and takes the tray back. "Off you go."|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|"Next time," she says, as a fact, not a question.}
opt.right.say.1 [afe44c] = {“谁不是呢，”她说着把托盘拿了回去，“走吧。”|她点点头，这比训你一顿还难受。|“下次，”她说——是陈述，不是询问。}
# en: Mend the worst one yourself
opt.up.label [0b5686] = 亲手治好最重的那个
# en: {You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. "Neat," she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.|The student with no eyebrows is the worst case. You coax them halfway back. Amaranth finishes the job, and writes your name on tomorrow's tray as well.}
opt.up.say.1 [21db53] = {你轻声一念，一道微光合上了裂开的指节。阿玛兰斯端详了很久。“利落，”她说——这是她嘴里最高的评价。|扭伤在你手下愈合。病人向阿玛兰斯道谢，阿玛兰斯指了指你。|那个没了眉毛的学生最棘手。你哄回来了一半。阿玛兰斯收了尾，把你的名字也写上了明天的托盘。}

@event amaranth_orders
# en: Doctor's Orders
title [abe905] = 医嘱
# en: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. "You look like a draught of bad decisions," she says. "Bed."|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. "I can hear you coughing from the infirmary."|"Bed," says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}
text [321559] = {阿玛兰斯修女竟然主动来找你了，这种事从没发生在任何人身上。“你看起来像一剂馊主意，”她说，“上床。”|又是阿玛兰斯，抱着胳膊站在你门口。“我在医务室都听得见你咳嗽。”|门还没完全打开，阿玛兰斯就说：“上床。”}
# en: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.
scene [a0e5b7] = 一位治疗师抱着胳膊站在宿舍门口，一名脸色苍白的学生正抱着一摞书，半个身子下了床。
# en: Go to bed as ordered
opt.left.label [c01d90] = 遵医嘱，上床
# en: {You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}
opt.left.say.1 [42f270] = {你睡了十四个小时。她来看了你两次，两次都在窗台上留了一瓶补剂。|两天的肉汤和无聊。熬过去，你又像个人了。|你争取再读一章。你输了，睡觉。}
# en: "I'm fine, honestly."
opt.right.label [9142ac] = “我没事，真的。”
# en: {"Nobody who says 'honestly' is," she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.|"Bed," she says again, as if you hadn't spoken. You don't go. By the weekend you wish you had.}
opt.right.say.1 [091428] = {“说‘真的’的人都有事，”她说完就走了。到了周四，你果然有事。|她在一张卡片上写了点什么就走了。后来发现那是你的名字，写在她的名单上。|“床，”她又说了一遍，好像你什么也没说。你没去。到了周末，你后悔了。}

@event amaranth_trust
# en: The Cupboard Key
title [11b16f] = 柜子钥匙
# en: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. "I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood," she says. "You don't." She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.
text [8d3c0e] = 阿玛兰斯修女随手关上储藏室的门。“冬至那几天我不在，见习治疗师一见血就晕，”她说，“你不会。”她递来补剂柜的钥匙，还有一页她自己写的愈合咒笔记。
# en: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.
scene [f064f3] = 夜里的医务室储藏室，只点着一盏灯，一位治疗师把一把小铜钥匙按进学生掌心，四周的架子上全是贴好标签的瓶子。
# en: Take the key and the notes
opt.left.label [bdfda9] = 收下钥匙和笔记
# en: "Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody," she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.
opt.left.say.1 [f5ce15] = “能治的治，治不了的送来给我，对谁都别撒谎，”她说。那页笔记是写给一个她认定会擅长此道的人的。你读了两遍，拼命想成为那样的人。
# en: "I'm not ready for that."
opt.right.label [5088da] = “我还没准备好。”
# en: She looks at you for a long moment. "Good answer. Wrong, but good." She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.
opt.right.say.1 [a29e05] = 她看了你好一会儿。“好回答。错了，但是好回答。”钥匙她收回去了，笔记还是给了你。

@event brask_shovel
# en: Hands Wanted
title [82086c] = 缺人手
# en: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|"Ah," says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}
text [25a91e] = {你路过马厩时，布拉斯克教授递过来一把铲子。她一句话没说。她身后的鹰头马身有翼兽倒是说了，而且很大声。|你还没进门，布拉斯克就把铲子递了过来。|“啊，”布拉斯克说，铲子已经在你手里了。} {狮鹫幼崽在闹脾气。|猫头鹰们对新铺的稻草颇有意见。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽吃了一只手套，可能是某位教授的。}
# en: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.
scene [b7d66c] = 一位饱经风霜、系着皮围裙的教授在马厩门口把铲子递给一名学生，一只鹰头马身有翼兽在隔栏里狐疑地看着。
# en: Muck out the stalls
opt.left.label [6ef185] = 清理马厩
# en: {An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, "The grey one likes you," which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}
opt.left.say.1 [e1fc8d] = {一个小时的稻草和沉默。最后布拉斯克说：“那只灰的喜欢你。”这比她这学期对任何人说的都多。|她给你讲了一个关于蝎尾狮、一只水桶和一位主教的故事。结尾是被咬了一口和一个道理。道理你没听清。|你走时，那只鹰头马身有翼兽向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克假装没看见。}
# en: Decline, politely
opt.right.label [96c3eb] = 客气地推辞
# en: {"Suit yourself," she says, and takes the shovel back. "The straw won't mind."|"The hippogriff doesn't want to either," says Brask. "It helps anyway."|You hand the shovel back. Brask takes it, and the hippogriff looks at your gloves with interest. You leave quickly.}
opt.right.say.1 [022ce5] = {“随你，”她说，把铲子拿了回去，“稻草不会介意的。”|“那只鹰头马身有翼兽也不想干，”布拉斯克说，“可它照样帮忙。”|你把铲子递了回去。布拉斯克接过来，那只鹰头马身有翼兽饶有兴趣地盯着你的手套。你赶紧走了。}
# en: Ask the stalls what they need
opt.up.label [30eec0] = 问问它们需要什么
# en: {The hippogriffs want the window open and the owls want it shut. You negotiate. Brask watches you with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|The griffon chick tells you, at length, about a grievance. You pass it on. Brask nods. "She's right, actually."|The hippogriff confesses to the glove, more or less, and you negotiate its return. Brask takes it back, damp, and says nothing, which from her is thanks.}
opt.up.say.1 [d44e9b] = {鹰头马身有翼兽们要开窗，猫头鹰们要关窗。你从中斡旋。布拉斯克抱着胳膊看着你，脸上几乎有点笑意。|狮鹫幼崽向你絮絮叨叨地诉了一大通委屈。你转告了。布拉斯克点点头：“其实它说得对。”|那只鹰头马身有翼兽差不多承认了手套的事，你谈妥了归还。布拉斯克接过湿漉漉的手套，什么也没说，对她来说这就是道谢。}

@event brask_night_owl
# en: The Sick Owl
title [a56a50] = 生病的猫头鹰
# en: Brask stops you in the corridor and falls into step without asking, which is a first. "The old barn owl won't eat," she says. "Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet." She is looking at you.
text [56bef6] = 布拉斯克在走廊里拦住你，招呼也不打就和你并肩走，这还是头一回。“那只老仓鸮不肯吃东西，”她说，“今晚得有人陪着她。得是个安静的人。”她看着你。
# en: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.
scene [939266] = 夜里昏暗的马厩阁楼，一名学生盘腿坐在草窝旁，一只生病的仓鸮盖着毯子睡着，提灯拧得很暗。
# en: Sit up with the owl
opt.left.label [2fd221] = 守着猫头鹰熬夜
# en: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.
opt.left.say.1 [bae058] = 你在阁楼里坐到天亮，一滴一滴往猫头鹰喙里喂水。凌晨四点，她从你指间叼走了一只老鼠。早上，布拉斯克盯着空碗看了很久，然后在你肩上狠狠拍了一下。
# en: Send her to Sister Amaranth
opt.right.label [53c847] = 让她去找阿玛兰斯修女
# en: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.
opt.right.say.1 [4f82b4] = 阿玛兰斯看看猫头鹰，又看看你，说她不治猫头鹰。然后她陪了它一整夜。布拉斯克不知该作何感想，你也一样。

@event brask_trust
# en: Old Courtesies
title [39af84] = 古老的礼节
# en: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood one afternoon. "Most people talk at animals," she says. "A few talk to them. Fewer listen." She bows, very low, to something in the trees. "Do as I do. I'll teach you the rest."
text [5297d4] = 一天下午，布拉斯克带你到低语森林边。“大多数人对动物说话，”她说，“少数人和它们说话。更少的人肯听。”她朝树林里的什么东西深深鞠了一躬。“照我做。剩下的我来教。”
# en: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.
scene [5eaec1] = 黄昏的森林边缘，一位教授和一名学生向半隐在树间的一头巨大雄鹿深深鞠躬。
# en: Bow, and learn
opt.left.label [80ee0e] = 鞠躬，然后学
# en: It takes until full dark, bent double until your back aches, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then, faintly, you understand a word. It is "rude". Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.
opt.left.say.1 [3382a7] = 一直学到天全黑，你弯着腰弯到背都酸了，大部分时间还有个庞然大物在你脖子后面喘气。然后，隐约地，你听懂了一个词：“没礼貌”。布拉斯克大笑了一声——就是她一学期一次的那种。
# en: Keep your distance
opt.right.label [0141a6] = 保持距离
# en: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.
opt.right.say.1 [2e5c6a] = 你站在小路边看着。她没有勉强你。回去的路上她讲了一个小时的蹄子护理，你怀疑这是一种体贴。

@event vey_pop_quiz
# en: Is It There?
title [5bb9f5] = 它在那儿吗？
# en: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. "Only one of them is real," she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. "There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't."|"Good morning," says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}
text [b25450] = {维伊教授端着两杯茶站在走廊里，递给你一杯。“只有一杯是真的，”她和颜悦色地说。|维伊在楼梯上拦住你。“你耳朵后面有枚硬币。也可能没有。”|“早上好，”维伊说，而你正要推开的那扇门，可能是一堵墙。} {她看上去已经很高兴了。|你身后有个学生撞上了什么东西。|她在哼歌。}
# en: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.
scene [ad6db0] = 一位系着紫罗兰色围巾的教授笑着倚在走廊墙边，手里端着一杯茶，旁边窗台上还有一杯，完美得有点过分。
# en: Look closely
opt.left.label [66d2dd] = 仔细看
# en: {You pick the real one. Vey raises an eyebrow. "Insufferable," she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. "Ah," says Vey. "You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it."|You lay your hand flat on it first. It's a door. Vey stops humming. "Pity," she says, delighted.}
opt.left.say.1 [145cd8] = {你挑中了真的那个。维伊挑了挑眉。“讨厌鬼，”她笑逐颜开地说。|你捕捉到了边缘那一丝微光。“啊，”维伊说，“你一直在留心。坏习惯。保持住。”|你先把手掌贴了上去。是门。维伊不哼了。“可惜，”她说，高兴得很。}
# en: {You reach for the real one and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. "It's usually the other one," she says, kindly, which is worse.|You walk confidently into what turns out to be a wall. Vey hums on, very pleased, while you hold your nose.}
opt.left.say.2 [a8dd9c] = {你伸手去拿真的那个，手指却合在了空气上。维伊对此相当享受。|错了。“通常都是另一个，”她和气地说，这更糟。|你信心十足地走了过去，结果那是一堵墙。你捂着鼻子，维伊继续哼着歌，非常满意。}
# en: Play along and get it wrong
opt.right.label [e10069] = 配合她，故意猜错
# en: {You choose the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. "Either you're bad at this, or very good," she says. "I'll find out."|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.|You walk into it on purpose, braced for a wall. It's a door, and you fall through it. Vey hums all the way down the corridor.}
opt.right.say.1 [f007d2] = {你故意、而且声情并茂地选了假的那个。维伊眯起眼睛。“要么你很不擅长这个，要么你非常擅长，”她说，“我会弄清楚的。”|你让她赢了。她知道。她也让你知道她知道。|你故意撞上去，准备好了撞墙。结果是门，你一头栽了进去。维伊哼着歌走完了整条走廊。}

@event vey_revue
# en: The Faculty Revue
title [22db23] = 教职员汇演
# en: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. "I need someone to play a king who isn't there," she says. "You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine."
text [551685] = 维伊教授在排演教职员汇演——教授们为慈善出丑的那种。“我需要一个人演一位并不在场的国王，”她说，“你会很出色。你会演砸。哪样都行。”
# en: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.
scene [44137b] = 大礼堂里一座挂着紫罗兰色幕布的小舞台，一位教授在指导一名拿着纸王冠的紧张学生，台下正在摆椅子。
# en: Take the part
opt.left.label [e6818e] = 接下这个角色
# en: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says "Hm" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.
opt.left.say.1 [d507d2] = 你把一位不在场的国王演得气势恢宏。前排的索恩说了两声“嗯”，教职员们一致认为这相当于起立鼓掌。
# en: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.
opt.left.say.2 [cd7f8a] = 你忘了台词，现编了新的，结果大家一致认为你是整场最精彩的部分——只是原因不太对。维伊把那顶纸王冠留在了她的书桌上。
# en: Hide in the wings
opt.right.label [f0270b] = 躲到侧幕
# en: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.
opt.right.say.1 [d2705a] = 你负责拉幕。汇演在没有国王的情况下照常进行，维伊说这正是重点所在。

@event vey_trust
# en: The Mirror Trick
title [2b6124] = 镜像戏法
# en: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. "The trick isn't making a copy," she says. "It's knowing which of you to send. Want to learn it?"
text [a3e14c] = 维伊课后留下你，把一面高高的镜子朝你倾过来。你的倒影从镜子里走出来，坐到了一张桌子上。“诀窍不在于造一个复制品，”她说，“而在于知道该派哪个你出去。想学吗？”
# en: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.
scene [30e560] = 黄昏时空荡荡的教室，一面高大的镜子两侧站着两个一模一样的学生，一位教授坐在中间的桌子上轻轻鼓掌。
# en: Learn it
opt.left.label [267092] = 学
# en: It takes a week. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, "Now you know why I don't do this in public." You are not sure which of you walked home.
opt.left.say.1 [9e2416] = 学了一周。最后一晚，你的倒影不肯回去了。维伊边喝茶边把它劝了回去，然后说：“现在你知道我为什么不在人前用这个了。”走回去的是你们中的哪一个，你也说不准。
# en: Keep your own face, thanks
opt.right.label [78e875] = 谢了，我还是用自己的脸
# en: "Sensible," she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.
opt.right.say.1 [28577d] = “明智，”她说，有点失望。她转而教你怎么识破别人在用这招。结果发现，用的人还真不少。

@event marisol_intro
# en: A Dry Patch of Air
title [7b3a88] = 一小片干燥的空气
# en: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. "It's not hard," says Professor Marisol. "You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like."
text [86e87f] = 整片湖都在下雨，只有船屋台阶上的一个圆圈例外：一个戴着湿透帽子的女人在那里读诗，浑身干爽。她抬起头。“不难，”玛丽索尔教授说，“只要请水绕开就行。想坐的话就坐吧。”
# en: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.
scene [765fe2] = 湖岸大雨，一个女人坐在船屋台阶上读书，头顶是一圈完全干燥的空气，雨滴像绕过玻璃一样从四周滑落。
# en: Sit and ask how
opt.left.label [9059dd] = 坐下，问她怎么做到的
# en: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute, and then it remembers you. "There," she says, and goes back to the poem.
opt.left.say.1 [806739] = 她把这解释成一场对话。水显然是愿意的。一首诗读完时，雨也绕着你走了，差不多一分钟，然后它又想起了你。“好了，”她说，接着读她的诗。
# en: Leave her to her book
opt.right.label [1f57ac] = 别打扰她读书
# en: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.
opt.right.say.1 [b3911a] = 你点点头，跑去躲雨。身后，她对着空无一人的湖岸朗读一首诗，写的是一场每年都回来看看你变了没有的暴风雨。

@event marisol_cold_water
# en: Cold Water Is Good for You
title [c84271] = 冷水对你有好处
# en: {Professor Marisol is in the lake, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. "It's lovely once you're in," she calls. It is not lovely.|"You came!" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}
text [c110b4] = {玛丽索尔教授站在齐腰深的湖里，朝你挥手，仿佛这再正常不过。|又是玛丽索尔，又在湖里。“下来就舒服了！”她喊。一点也不舒服。|“你来啦！”玛丽索尔在水里喊。你本来只是路过。} {湖边结了冰。|一只苍鹭正以同行的敬意看着她。|她一只手举着书，书一点没湿。}
# en: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.
scene [2bea30] = 黎明的薄雾笼罩着平静的湖面，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里，朝码头上冻得发抖的学生快活地挥手。
# en: Wade in
opt.left.label [f7f4b9] = 蹚下水
# en: {She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}
opt.left.say.1 [5536c9] = {她教你感受湖的呼吸。你感受不到自己的脚。她说两者都很正常。|你请水升起来，它真的在你膝边升起了一点。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。|等你爬上岸，你已经不发抖了。她说这是好兆头，阿玛兰斯会说这是坏兆头。}
# en: Wave back from the jetty
opt.right.label [c55e26] = 在码头上朝她挥手
# en: {She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|"Coward," she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.|"Next time, then," she calls, and goes back to her book, held perfectly dry above the water.}
opt.right.say.1 [154360] = {她毫不介意地又挥了挥手，沉了下去。|“胆小鬼，”她快活地喊。你觉得这样挺好。|“那就下次，”她喊道，又看起了书，书在水面上举得干干的。}
# en: Raise a wave to say hello
opt.up.label [4bb1cd] = 掀个浪打声招呼
# en: {A small, neat wave rolls across the lake and taps her on the shoulder. She laughs so hard she sinks.|Your wave arrives slightly late and very large. Marisol rides it in to shore, delighted.|Your wave rolls in neatly and stops just short of her book. Marisol applauds with her free hand.}
opt.up.say.1 [9c2c82] = {一道小小的、整齐的浪越过湖面，拍了拍她的肩。她笑得沉了下去。|你的浪来得有点晚，也有点大。玛丽索尔乘着它冲上了岸，高兴极了。|你的浪整整齐齐地涌过去，正好停在她的书前面。玛丽索尔用空着的那只手鼓掌。}

@event marisol_storm
# en: Weather
title [eab633] = 天气
# en: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. "Nobody learns tides on a calm day," she shouts over the wind. "Coming?"
text [837719] = 一年里最猛的暴风雨中，有人敲你的窗。玛丽索尔教授站在外面，头发乱飞，满脸笑容。“风平浪静的日子里谁也学不会潮汐，”她在风里大喊，“来不来？”
# en: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.
scene [69a9a9] = 漆黑湖面上的狂暴风雨，一位教授和一名学生站在岩石岬角上高举双臂，身后闪电劈开天空。
# en: Go out into it
opt.left.label [d9d690] = 冲进风雨里
# en: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.
opt.left.say.1 [b3da39] = 在岬角上、在轰鸣声中，你把一道浪整整挡住了三秒。玛丽索尔开心得尖叫。你浑身湿透地回家，却像一盏点亮的灯。
# en: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.
opt.left.say.2 [b1eb0b] = 湖水把你掀翻在地。玛丽索尔揪着你的领子把你拖出来，一半在笑一半在道歉，一路把你送进了医务室。
# en: Close the window
opt.right.label [486e14] = 关上窗
# en: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.
opt.right.say.1 [457fde] = 她耸耸肩，毫不在意地跑进雨里。你躺着听了一夜湖水，想知道自己错过了什么。

@event marisol_trust
# en: The Last Line
title [66ff3e] = 最后一行
# en: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. "I never remember the last lines," she says. "You will. The tidecalling's in the margins; I wrote it there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore."
text [dc1bc8] = 玛丽索尔把她那本旧的咏水诗集塞到你手里。“我总记不住最后一行，”她说，“你会记住的。唤潮术写在页边，是我在你这个年纪时写的。去湖边读。在湖边读效果更好。”
# en: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.
scene [41e145] = 日落时宁静的湖边，一位教授把一本夹着丝带的旧诗集递给学生，眼前的湖水平静如镜。
# en: Read it on the shore
opt.left.label [0950d9] = 去湖边读
# en: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.
opt.left.say.1 [2dd6f7] = 你站在没过脚踝的水里一直读到天黑。翻到最后一页时，湖水轻轻地回应了你，一直传到对岸。你记住了最后一行。你替她把它写了上去。
# en: Give it back; it's hers
opt.right.label [404c00] = 还给她，这是她的
# en: She looks at the book and then at you. "Nobody's ever given it back," she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.
opt.right.say.1 [fcc45a] = 她看看书，又看看你。“从来没人还回来过，”她说，然后亲自给你读了那首诗。她忘了最后一行。你告诉了她。

@event mabel_intro
# en: The Way You Open a Door
title [3e82a5] = 你推门的样子
# en: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. "Homesick," says Mabel, "with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal."
text [0c0a87] = 门上的铃铛响了。柜台后的女人抬起头，打量了一下你进门的样子，你还没开口，她已经伸手去拿一罐茶了。“想家，”梅布尔说，“外加一份考试。坐窗边吧，小宝贝。”
# en: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.
scene [436b7b] = 温馨的村庄茶馆，门上挂着铃铛，一名学生从寒风中走进来，柜台后的女人已经伸手去拿一罐茶。
# en: Sit by the window
opt.left.label [8f510d] = 坐到窗边
# en: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. "First visit," she says. "After that I charge double."
opt.left.say.1 [e7981f] = 茶恰到好处，你说不出为什么。她端来一块你没点的蛋糕，没收钱。“第一次来，”她说，“以后收双倍。”
# en: Say you'll come back another time
opt.right.label [64684d] = 说你改天再来
# en: She looks at you over the counter. "Come back when you've got time to sit," she says. "You'll know when."
opt.right.say.1 [088ded] = 她隔着柜台看着你。“等你有空坐下了再来，”她说，“到时候你自己会知道。”

@event mabel_parcel
# en: A Tin From Mabel
title [89fe3c] = 梅布尔寄来的茶罐
# en: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says "FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR."|Another tin from Mabel. The label says "FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS."|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only "YOU KNOW."} {There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}
text [6c5e1e] = {驳船送来一个小罐子，上面是梅布尔圆圆的字迹写着你的名字。标签上写着“治那声隔着门我都听见了的咳嗽”。|梅布尔又寄来一罐。标签上写着“治这一周不管是什么的那些事”。|梅布尔的茶罐已经占了你一整层架子。今天的标签只写着“你懂的”。} {绳子下面压着一朵干紫罗兰。|闻起来有蜂蜜和柴烟的味道。|盖子上用胶带粘着一块饼干。}
# en: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.
scene [7e98e5] = 宿舍枕头上放着一小罐用牛皮纸和细绳包好的茶，上面贴着手写标签，绳子下压着一朵干花。
# en: Bring her something back
opt.left.label [1f40bf] = 给她捎点东西回去
# en: {You bring her a jar of honey from the market. She calls it bribery and puts it on the shelf behind the counter.|You bring her flowers from the village stall. "Don't," she says. "Drink it." You do. It works.|You bring her a tin of your own, labelled "YOU KNOW". She laughs until she has to sit down, and keeps it.}
opt.left.say.1 [5f75b3] = {你从集市给她带了一罐蜂蜜。她说这是贿赂，把它摆上了柜台后面的架子。|你从镇上的花摊给她带了束花。“别谢，”她说，“喝。”你喝了。管用。|你给她带去一罐自己的，标签上写着“你知道”。她笑得不得不坐下来，然后收下了。}
# en: Send it back with a note
opt.right.label [7dbd32] = 附张字条寄回去
# en: {She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says "STUBBORN."|You send it back with a note that says "I KNOW". It comes back with two biscuits taped to the lid.}
opt.right.say.1 [9369ce] = {她又寄了回来，附上一张更长的字条。你放弃抵抗，喝了。|茶罐搭下一班驳船回来了。标签现在写着“犟”。|你寄了回去，附一张字条写着“我知道”。它又回来了，盖子上粘了两块饼干。}

@event mabel_cup
# en: Your Cup on the Hook
title [8777bb] = 挂钩上你的杯子
# en: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. "Regulars," she says, as if that settled it.
text [2202c5] = 梅布尔的柜台后面有一排挂钩，挂着杯子，每只都画着一个字母。你从没问过它们是谁的。今天，你看着梅布尔挂上一只新杯子，上面画着你名字的首字母。“常客，”她说，好像这就说明了一切。
# en: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.
scene [ea24f5] = 茶馆柜台后挂钩上一排不成套的杯子，一个女人正挂上一只新杯子，上面画着一名学生名字的首字母。
# en: Ask whose the others are
opt.left.label [858fea] = 问问其他杯子是谁的
# en: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. "And you," she says. Something in your chest unknots.
opt.left.say.1 [8a1266] = 她一只一只告诉你：一个渔夫，一位已故的校长，一个后来成了公爵夫人、至今仍写信来的姑娘。“还有你，”她说。你胸口有什么东西松开了。
# en: Say thank you and sit down
opt.right.label [d9af96] = 道声谢，坐下
# en: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.
opt.right.say.1 [77da72] = 你坐下。她用你的杯子给你端来茶。你不需要再说什么，她也一样。

@event grimsby_intro
# en: Not That One
title [40767f] = 不是那本
# en: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says "Ah!" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. "Not what you asked for," says Mr Grimsby. "What you'll need. You'll see."
text [cb47fb] = 你要一本教科书。梯子上的老人热情洋溢地“啊！”了一声，递给你一本灯塔史。“不是你要的，”格林斯比先生说，“是你需要的。你会明白的。”
# en: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.
scene [ee1fec] = 拥挤的书店里书架东倒西歪，一位年迈的书商站在梯子上把一本书递给下面的学生，学生手里捏着一张书单。
# en: Buy the lighthouse book
opt.left.label [aed25d] = 买下那本灯塔书
# en: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. "They always come back," he says. "Lighthouses."
opt.left.say.1 [ae1dbc] = 你在回程的驳船上读了它。第四章恰好有你论文缺的那个证明。你回去告诉他，他只是点点头。“它们总会回来的，”他说，“灯塔。”
# en: Say you'll think about it
opt.right.label [b4769e] = 说你考虑一下
# en: He puts the lighthouses back on the shelf, sadly, like a doctor whose prescription has been refused. "It'll keep," he says. "So will you."
opt.right.say.1 [9586aa] = 他把那本讲灯塔的书放回书架，满脸遗憾，就像一个医生的处方被拒绝了。“它跑不了，”他说，“你也跑不了。”

@event grimsby_parcel
# en: The Wrong Book, Posted
title [da6a85] = 寄来的错书
# en: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says "Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G."|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}
text [79ef26] = {格林斯比书店寄来一个包裹。里面是一本你没订的书，附着一张字条，写着“想到了你。方便时再付。A.G.”|格林斯比先生又寄来一个包裹。这本讲的是鳗鱼。|格林斯比的包裹现在每月都来，像天气一样。} {闻起来有雨水和烟斗的味道。|封面里夹着一张上个世纪的车票。|字条写在一张菜谱的背面。}
# en: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.
scene [443ef4] = 图书馆书桌上放着一个用牛皮纸和细绳捆着的包裹，一名学生正拆开一本古怪的旧书，邻座的猫在一旁看着。
# en: Keep it and pay him
opt.left.label [37a868] = 留下，付钱给他
# en: {It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.|You pay him, and try the recipe on the back of the note. The book and the cake are both strange, and both good.}
opt.left.say.1 [b466d3] = {果然是本错书，正如承诺；而书里某处，正是你要的那一页。向来如此。|你出于礼貌开始读，读完却是出于别的原因。|你付了钱，还试了字条背面的菜谱。书和蛋糕都很怪，也都很好。}
# en: Send it back with the carrier
opt.right.label [46b3c6] = 交给邮差退回去
# en: {The carrier takes it back for nothing, grumbling. Grimsby sends a cheerful note that says "Next time, then."|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.|You send it back. Next month's parcel arrives anyway, as if nothing had happened, with a new recipe on the back of the note.}
opt.right.say.1 [4792f3] = {邮差骂骂咧咧地把它免费带了回去。格林斯比寄来一张欢快的字条，写着“那就下次吧”。|他立刻把它寄了回来，外加一本更错的书。两本你都留下了。|你寄了回去。下个月的包裹照样寄到，好像什么都没发生过，字条背面换了一份新菜谱。}

@event grimsby_mind_the_shop
# en: Mind the Shop
title [2eaf46] = 照看书店
# en: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. "You know where everything is," he says, which is untrue. "Better than me," he adds, which is.
text [355c82] = 格林斯比先生从梯子上摔了下来（不严重，落在了诗歌区），需要有人替他照看书店一周。“你知道东西都在哪儿，”他说，这不是真的。“比我清楚，”他补了一句，这倒是真的。
# en: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.
scene [5a432f] = 黄昏时一名学生独自站在拥挤的旧书店柜台后，柜台上放着一串钥匙，一位顾客透过满是灰尘的橱窗往里张望。
# en: Mind the shop
opt.left.label [d31c26] = 照看书店
# en: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.
opt.left.say.1 [60e74c] = 你卖出了十一本书，全是错的，而每位顾客都回来道谢。拄着拐杖的格林斯比说你有天分。他是当作警告说的。
# en: "I can't, I'm sorry."
opt.right.label [c71ad9] = “我做不到，抱歉。”
# en: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.
opt.right.say.1 [c09b7c] = 他干脆歇业一周，门上挂了块牌子：“看灯塔去了”。没人知道是哪一座。

@event pell_intro
# en: A Thousand Drawers
title [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉
# en: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. "Willow and feverfew," says Old Pell. "Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen."
text [b95382] = 你想买点治头痛的药。梯子上的老人停下来，低声数了数抽屉，下来时手里恰好拿着一小包纸。“柳树皮加小白菊，”老佩尔说，“三撮。不是四撮。四撮是给不听话的人的。”
# en: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.
scene [7dc69c] = 一位老药剂师站在一面贴满标签的小抽屉墙前的梯子上，从眼镜上方俯视着柜台前的学生。
# en: Ask why not four
opt.left.label [3eee3f] = 问他为什么不是四撮
# en: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. He's so pleased someone asked that he forgets to charge you. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.
opt.left.say.1 [38fb49] = 他讲了很久，还在柜台的灰上画了图。有人肯问，他高兴得忘了收钱。你带着药、一场讲座带来的新头痛，以及另外九种东西的剂量离开了。
# en: Pay and go
opt.right.label [9ac792] = 付钱走人
# en: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. The headache is gone before the quay, and you are back within a month.
opt.right.say.1 [b2eaf4] = 你走时他朝门口点点头，仿佛把你记进了一本“会回来的人”的账。还没到码头，头就不疼了；不到一个月，你果然回来了。

@event pell_count_drops
# en: Count Them Aloud
title [6a0261] = 大声数出来
# en: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. "Forty. Aloud." He watches your lips, not the vial.|"Forty," says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}
text [2f8c1a] = {老佩尔把一支滴管放进你手里。“四十滴。数出声。”他盯着的是你的嘴唇，不是药瓶。|“四十，”佩尔说，然后站到你身后。你能听见他在低声跟着数。|佩尔开始把难配的酊剂留给你了。这就是他表达信任的方式。} {一个女儿在柜台那边翻了个白眼。|有马车经过时，抽屉们咔嗒作响。|三百号抽屉里有什么东西在嗡嗡响。}
# en: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.
scene [6dff31] = 一位老药剂师站在木柜台前，把一支玻璃滴管悬在药瓶上方，身旁的学生掰着手指数数，身后是一整面贴着标签的小抽屉。
# en: Count, aloud, every drop
opt.left.label [f6db0f] = 一滴一滴大声数
# en: {Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then "Again." You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.|Forty, of a tincture that fights back. Pell holds the vial to the light and puts it in drawer three hundred. The humming stops.}
opt.left.say.1 [426397] = {正好四十滴。佩尔整整一分钟没说话，然后说“再来”。你相当确定那是在夸你。|数到三十一你乱了，没等人说就从头再来。佩尔赞许地哼了一声。|四十滴，还是一种会反抗的酊剂。佩尔把药瓶举到光下看了看，放进了第三百号抽屉。嗡嗡声停了。}
# en: Eyeball it; it's close enough
opt.right.label [e3a574] = 目测一下，差不多就行
# en: {It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. "Luck," says Pell. "Don't."|The hard tincture forgives you, today. Pell sniffs it and puts it away without comment, which is worse than comment.}
opt.right.say.1 [5f639d] = {对了。佩尔查了三遍，对它居然是对的这件事明显很恼火。|差不多，今天恰好就是刚好。“运气，”佩尔说，“别指望。”|今天，这难缠的酊剂放了你一马。佩尔闻了闻，一句评价也没有就收了起来，这比评价更糟。}
# en: {Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.|Too many, of the hard one. Drawer three hundred hums louder, and Pell keeps the dropper for the rest of the week.}
opt.right.say.2 [9adae6] = {四十三滴。佩尔一言不发地倒掉，把滴管又递给你。这份沉默持续了整个班。|多了。一个女儿赶在他看见前改好了。他还是看见了。|多了，偏偏是难缠的那种。第三百号抽屉嗡得更响了，佩尔这一星期剩下的日子都没再把滴管给你。}

@event pell_daughters
# en: Pell & Daughters
title [e047f1] = 佩尔父女
# en: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. "Well?" says Pell.
text [5b6af3] = 佩尔的女儿们想要一块新招牌、更醒目的标签，还想把柜台转过来朝着门。佩尔想要五十年前的样子。三个人一齐转向你——铺子里唯一不是家里人的那个。“怎么说？”佩尔问。
# en: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.
scene [d34c2c] = 一位老药剂师和两个成年的女儿隔着柜台为一块新漆的招牌争吵，一名学生尴尬地站在中间。
# en: Side with the daughters
opt.left.label [3b974c] = 站在女儿们一边
# en: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.
opt.left.say.1 [fb675a] = 佩尔跺着脚进了里屋。一周后，新招牌挂了上去。招牌下面，用小小的旧字，依然写着“佩尔”。他板着脸指给你看，好像是你问起似的。
# en: "The drawers have never been wrong."
opt.right.label [d09cc9] = “抽屉从来没错过。”
# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a bonus and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.
opt.right.say.1 [dc138f] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔给了你一笔奖金，女儿们把最糟的盘点全派给了你。大家都觉得这很公平。
# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell presses a bottle of his best tonic on you, and the daughters give you a look that lasts a month.
opt.right.say.2 [3fae36] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔硬塞给你一瓶他最好的补剂，女儿们给了你一个持续一个月的眼神。

@event grisk_pleasantries
# en: Pleasantries
title [3f3d10] = 寒暄
# en: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. "My friend," he says. "Do sit. I'm not working."|Grisk, at the corner table again. "How is the purse?" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): "Ah, my favourite client," purrs Grisk.|"Ah, my favourite non-client," purrs Grisk.}} {His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}
text [30a799] = {格里斯克在镀金蝾螈酒馆的角落桌边举起一只小酒杯。“我的朋友，”他说，“请坐。我今天不营业。”|格里斯克又坐在角落那张桌子。“钱袋还好吗？”他问，听起来像是真心关切。|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): “啊，我最喜欢的客户，”格里斯克轻声说。|“啊，我最喜欢的非客户，”格里斯克轻声说。}} {他的账簿一直合着，显然是故意的。|他正非常缓慢地吃着一颗橄榄。|老板娘朝你投来警告的一瞥。}
# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.
scene [83bdbd] = 低矮横梁的酒馆角落，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精向一名学生举起一只小酒杯，手肘边放着一本厚厚的账簿。
# en: Sit for one drink
opt.left.label [b539a6] = 坐下喝一杯
# en: {He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}
opt.left.say.1 [aecf5e] = {他谈起钱来就像别人谈天气，你在一小时里学到的比一学期暗影理论还多。|他告诉你村里哪些铺子遇到了麻烦，以及原因。听着像闲话，其实是一堂课。|他付了酒钱，什么也没记——这是他做过最让人不安的事。}
# en: Keep walking
opt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续走
# en: {"Another time," he calls after you. "I'm very patient."|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.|You keep walking. The landlady nods to you on your way out, very slightly.}
opt.right.say.1 [8ecc7b] = {“改天，”他在你身后喊，“我很有耐心。”|他照样举了杯。直到走出门，你都觉得那目光落在你两肩之间。|你继续往外走。经过时，老板娘冲你微微点了下头。}

@event grisk_tip
# en: A Tip, Between Friends
title [576ce2] = 朋友之间的消息
# en: Grisk slides a coin across the table. "Between friends," he murmurs. "The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know."
text [127226] = 格里斯克把一枚硬币推过桌面。“朋友之间，”他低声说，“驳船公司要买第二条驳船了。船夫的表亲这周会贱卖他的股份，因为他不知道。你可以知道。”
# en: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.
scene [95b950] = 一个地精隔着酒馆桌子凑到学生耳边低语，用一根手指把一枚金币推过木桌。
# en: Buy the share
opt.left.label [cddc5b] = 买下那份股份
# en: The share costs a fair few crowns.
opt.left.hint [af6e64] = 这份股份要花上不少克朗。
# en: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.
opt.left.say.1 [d08cfb] = 第二条驳船到了。股份翻了三倍。格里斯克隔着蝾螈酒馆朝你举杯。你觉得自己很有钱，又有点不舒服。
# en: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. "Risk," he says. "Delicious, and sometimes poison."
opt.left.say.2 [086af1] = 第二条驳船第一次渡湖就沉了。格里斯克很同情。“风险，”他说，“美味，有时也是毒药。”
# en: Leave the cousin his share
opt.right.label [f20d63] = 把股份留给那位表亲
# en: Grisk pockets the coin. "Honest," he says, as if diagnosing something. "It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired."
opt.right.say.1 [89bb79] = 格里斯克收起硬币。“老实，”他说，像在诊断什么病，“会好的。要么好不了，那你就会又穷又受人敬重。”
